1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,440
A tiny law office. Folding chairs. A

2
00:00:03,340 --> 00:00:04,600
will, and one sentence.

3
00:00:05,317 --> 00:00:07,757
I leave everything I own to the

4
00:00:07,657 --> 00:00:09,717
town library and the children's hospital.

5
00:00:10,622 --> 00:00:16,001
The total: $2,032,554.

6
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:20,060
Two million? Walter? Walter ate soup for

7
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:21,700
dinner. Every single night!

8
00:00:22,133 --> 00:00:24,333
His coat was held together with safety

9
00:00:24,233 --> 00:00:28,453
pins. Eleven of them. I counted.

10
00:00:33,096 --> 00:00:36,716
Walter was the school janitor. Same mop,

11
00:00:37,016 --> 00:00:38,976
same coat, same car.

12
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:43,320
So how? How does a janitor leave

13
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:44,060
two million dollars?

14
00:00:45,675 --> 00:00:48,795
Ms. Kowalski slides a tiny brass key

15
00:00:48,695 --> 00:00:53,215
across the table. Toward Lily.

16
00:00:54,126 --> 00:00:55,426
He left you something else.

17
00:00:56,090 --> 00:00:58,890
I'm Penny. These people are made up.

18
00:00:59,290 --> 00:01:01,850
Every number is real. Let's go back

19
00:01:01,750 --> 00:01:02,370
one week.

20
00:01:03,912 --> 00:01:08,351
Meet Lily. Twenty-eight. Rent due. Three maxed-out

21
00:01:08,252 --> 00:01:09,012
credit cards.

22
00:01:09,884 --> 00:01:12,044
I call them my three little dragons.

23
00:01:12,964 --> 00:01:16,204
They're sleeping. Please don't wake them.

24
00:01:17,277 --> 00:01:21,017
Every Sunday, Lily brought soup next door.

25
00:01:20,917 --> 00:01:23,017
To Walter.

26
00:01:24,023 --> 00:01:26,542
Honestly? I felt sorry for him.

27
00:01:27,067 --> 00:01:31,207
Flashback. Walter, in the safety-pin coat.

28
00:01:31,868 --> 00:01:33,908
Soup again? You know I make soup

29
00:01:33,808 --> 00:01:34,768
every night, right?

30
00:01:35,466 --> 00:01:37,806
Then why do you always take mine?

31
00:01:38,729 --> 00:01:41,048
Yours has chicken in it. Mine has

32
00:01:40,949 --> 00:01:41,509
opinions.

33
00:01:42,193 --> 00:01:44,133
On his shelf, a jar of coins

34
00:01:44,033 --> 00:01:46,565
labeled: Storm Jar.

35
00:01:46,795 --> 00:01:48,294
What's the jar for, Walter?

36
00:01:49,236 --> 00:01:51,496
Storms. Obviously. Eat your soup.

37
00:01:52,141 --> 00:01:55,661
Then, last Tuesday, nobody answered Walter's door.

38
00:01:56,314 --> 00:01:58,533
Back at the will reading, the door

39
00:01:58,434 --> 00:01:59,374
bangs open.

40
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:03,919
Nobody reads anything without me! Derek. Cousin.

41
00:02:04,220 --> 00:02:05,559
Closest living family.

42
00:02:06,253 --> 00:02:10,893
You visited him once. Christmas, 2011.

43
00:02:11,565 --> 00:02:13,805
And I brought a fruit basket. That's

44
00:02:13,705 --> 00:02:14,085
love.

45
00:02:14,685 --> 00:02:16,725
You're sitting in my chair, Derek.

46
00:02:17,389 --> 00:02:20,108
That money belongs to family. And whatever

47
00:02:20,009 --> 00:02:22,749
that little key opens? Also mine.

48
00:02:23,380 --> 00:02:25,960
Lily turns the key over. Stamped on

49
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:27,859
the back: Box 467.

50
00:02:28,577 --> 00:02:30,737
What does a man with eleven safety

51
00:02:30,637 --> 00:02:33,295
pins keep in a bank vault?

52
00:02:34,425 --> 00:02:37,345
Next morning. The vault. Derek tags along,

53
00:02:37,245 --> 00:02:38,305
uninvited.

54
00:02:38,753 --> 00:02:42,113
I'm here to supervise. As family.

55
00:02:42,740 --> 00:02:44,960
You're here because somebody said the word

56
00:02:44,860 --> 00:02:45,299
vault.

57
00:02:46,052 --> 00:02:47,311
And the lid opens.

58
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:51,280
One: a worn notebook. On the cover,

59
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:53,299
in pencil: Walter's Rules.

60
00:02:53,903 --> 00:02:59,363
Two: a yellowed newspaper. October 20th, 1987.

61
00:02:59,883 --> 00:03:02,684
The headline: STOCKS CRASH.

62
00:03:02,914 --> 00:03:05,534
Three: a fat stack of envelopes.

63
00:03:06,015 --> 00:03:09,555
Four: a birthday card. Sealed. With one

64
00:03:09,455 --> 00:03:10,174
name on it.

65
00:03:10,587 --> 00:03:12,207
That's... my name.

66
00:03:13,078 --> 00:03:16,118
Why keep a forty-year-old newspaper? Was he

67
00:03:16,018 --> 00:03:17,458
collecting bad news?

68
00:03:18,443 --> 00:03:20,843
And why is there a card for

69
00:03:20,743 --> 00:03:23,602
me? From nineteen ninety-eight?

70
00:03:24,569 --> 00:03:26,249
The notebook's first line:

71
00:03:27,005 --> 00:03:29,585
Read the envelopes first. The card goes

72
00:03:29,485 --> 00:03:32,585
last. Patience, kid. That's the whole trick.

73
00:03:33,297 --> 00:03:36,177
He's bossing me around. From a notebook.

74
00:03:37,171 --> 00:03:38,971
He was a very organized man.

75
00:03:39,566 --> 00:03:41,766
The answer to two million dollars is

76
00:03:41,666 --> 00:03:44,585
in this box. Lily opens the first

77
00:03:44,486 --> 00:03:45,066
envelope.

78
00:03:46,830 --> 00:03:49,510
Each one holds a bank receipt. And

79
00:03:49,410 --> 00:03:50,230
the same number.

80
00:03:50,886 --> 00:03:55,506
Three hundred dollars. January 1988. Three hundred.

81
00:03:56,866 --> 00:03:59,286
February. Three hundred. March...

82
00:04:00,279 --> 00:04:04,179
Into an index fund: one basket of

83
00:04:04,079 --> 00:04:07,559
America's five hundred biggest companies.

84
00:04:08,641 --> 00:04:14,561
467 envelopes. Not one month missed.

85
00:04:15,780 --> 00:04:23,220
467! Box 467! He picked the box

86
00:04:23,120 --> 00:04:24,420
number on purpose!

87
00:04:25,265 --> 00:04:27,425
Of course he did. That's very Walter.

88
00:04:28,181 --> 00:04:30,541
One from 1995 has a note:

89
00:04:31,235 --> 00:04:33,055
Car died on the highway today. Took

90
00:04:32,955 --> 00:04:34,975
the bus home. Envelope still goes in.

91
00:04:35,351 --> 00:04:37,371
Three hundred a month? I spend that

92
00:04:37,271 --> 00:04:39,151
on... actually, I don't know what on.

93
00:04:39,549 --> 00:04:40,769
That's usually the problem.

94
00:04:41,467 --> 00:04:44,687
Add them all up: $140,100.

95
00:04:45,737 --> 00:04:48,657
That's a lot of soup money. But

96
00:04:48,557 --> 00:04:50,357
it's not two million dollars.

97
00:04:51,263 --> 00:04:55,003
See? Missing money! Very suspicious. I smell

98
00:04:54,903 --> 00:04:55,723
a lawsuit.

99
00:04:56,296 --> 00:04:57,596
You smell my coffee.

100
00:04:58,300 --> 00:05:00,320
So where did the rest come from?

101
00:05:00,740 --> 00:05:02,620
The next clue was yellowing in that

102
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:02,860
box.

103
00:05:04,475 --> 00:05:08,735
The newspaper. October 20th, 1987.

104
00:05:09,618 --> 00:05:12,398
The day before, Black Monday, the stock

105
00:05:12,298 --> 00:05:16,298
market fell 20.5 percent. In one single

106
00:05:16,198 --> 00:05:16,798
day.

107
00:05:17,285 --> 00:05:20,465
Everyone panicked. In the margin, in pencil:

108
00:05:21,213 --> 00:05:23,573
Everybody's selling. So the stores are having

109
00:05:23,473 --> 00:05:25,133
a sale. I'm going shopping.

110
00:05:25,810 --> 00:05:28,970
That week, Walter opened his account. First

111
00:05:28,870 --> 00:05:31,330
envelope: January 1988.

112
00:05:32,194 --> 00:05:35,014
He started investing... during a crash?

113
00:05:36,155 --> 00:05:38,035
Turn the page. There's more.

114
00:05:38,599 --> 00:05:42,559
Taped inside: three receipts. Five thousand dollars

115
00:05:42,459 --> 00:05:44,979
each, on three terrible days.

116
00:05:45,743 --> 00:05:50,163
The 2002 crash. The 2009 crisis. The

117
00:05:50,063 --> 00:05:51,303
2020 shutdown.

118
00:05:52,106 --> 00:05:54,446
The Storm Jar! He said it was

119
00:05:54,346 --> 00:05:56,326
for storms. I thought he was joking!

120
00:05:57,368 --> 00:06:00,848
On the 2009 receipt, one line:

121
00:06:01,811 --> 00:06:05,171
Everyone thinks I'm crazy. Good. Crazy people

122
00:06:05,071 --> 00:06:06,111
get the best prices.

123
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:11,680
That 2002 five thousand is worth $73,299

124
00:06:11,580 --> 00:06:17,320
today. The 2009 one: $74,227.

125
00:06:18,488 --> 00:06:22,688
Even the 2020 one: $17,827.

126
00:06:23,912 --> 00:06:25,412
So he bought when everyone was scared?

127
00:06:25,812 --> 00:06:27,492
That's actually smart. I hate it.

128
00:06:27,886 --> 00:06:31,586
Three storms. Fifteen thousand dollars. Still nowhere

129
00:06:31,486 --> 00:06:32,696
near two million.

130
00:06:33,439 --> 00:06:35,639
Then Lily finds a pencil chart in

131
00:06:35,539 --> 00:06:36,059
the notebook.

132
00:06:36,783 --> 00:06:39,883
If you like money mysteries, subscribe, so

133
00:06:39,783 --> 00:06:41,063
you don't miss the next one.

134
00:06:42,445 --> 00:06:45,485
Every ten years, Walter wrote down his

135
00:06:45,385 --> 00:06:45,845
balance.

136
00:06:46,651 --> 00:06:51,431
1996, after nine years: $66,440.

137
00:06:53,253 --> 00:06:56,273
Nine years of soup. Feels like nothing's

138
00:06:56,173 --> 00:06:57,633
happening. Keep going.

139
00:06:58,157 --> 00:07:00,777
2006: $209,040.

140
00:07:01,933 --> 00:07:04,893
Car still runs. Coat still works. Keep

141
00:07:04,793 --> 00:07:05,313
going.

142
00:07:05,833 --> 00:07:11,473
2016, almost twenty-nine years in: $479,188.

143
00:07:12,650 --> 00:07:17,470
Half a million. Amazing. But still not

144
00:07:17,370 --> 00:07:18,250
two million.

145
00:07:19,173 --> 00:07:28,493
Then the last ten years. That $479,188

146
00:07:28,393 --> 00:07:30,973
grew more than four times bigger.

147
00:07:31,921 --> 00:07:35,801
76 percent of all the money appeared

148
00:07:35,701 --> 00:07:37,441
in the final ten years.

149
00:07:38,488 --> 00:07:42,908
76 percent? At the very end?

150
00:07:43,949 --> 00:07:48,129
That's compounding. Your money earns money. Then

151
00:07:48,029 --> 00:07:49,649
that money earns money too.

152
00:07:50,472 --> 00:07:52,992
It's a snowball. For years you're just

153
00:07:52,892 --> 00:07:55,412
pushing snow. Then one day it rolls

154
00:07:55,312 --> 00:07:56,152
by itself.

155
00:07:56,978 --> 00:07:58,538
Forty years to get rich, and he

156
00:07:58,438 --> 00:08:01,238
never spent it? That's not genius. That's

157
00:08:01,138 --> 00:08:01,478
crazy.

158
00:08:02,267 --> 00:08:03,987
Or maybe it's both?

159
00:08:04,835 --> 00:08:08,675
Derek marches straight to the courthouse. With

160
00:08:08,575 --> 00:08:10,675
a lawyer he found on a bus

161
00:08:10,575 --> 00:08:11,115
bench.

162
00:08:14,136 --> 00:08:18,116
Monday. A court letter lands on Ms.

163
00:08:18,236 --> 00:08:19,096
Kowalski's desk.

164
00:08:19,931 --> 00:08:22,771
Derek is contesting the will. He says

165
00:08:22,671 --> 00:08:24,491
Walter wasn't of sound mind.

166
00:08:25,243 --> 00:08:27,563
Nobody sane lives like that! Soup every

167
00:08:27,463 --> 00:08:30,123
night? Safety pins? With millions in the

168
00:08:30,023 --> 00:08:30,383
bank?

169
00:08:30,888 --> 00:08:33,488
He was clearly confused. And I was

170
00:08:33,388 --> 00:08:34,868
clearly his favorite cousin.

171
00:08:35,390 --> 00:08:37,590
You were his only cousin.

172
00:08:38,400 --> 00:08:40,581
Which makes me the favorite. That's math.

173
00:08:41,076 --> 00:08:44,616
Hearing's Friday, nine a.m. If the judge

174
00:08:44,516 --> 00:08:46,856
agrees, the will could be thrown out.

175
00:08:47,610 --> 00:08:49,150
And then who gets the money?

176
00:08:49,918 --> 00:08:53,038
Most likely the closest family. Derek.

177
00:08:53,702 --> 00:08:56,222
I'm thinking a boat. A big one.

178
00:08:56,362 --> 00:08:58,522
I'll name it after Walter. The Soup.

179
00:08:59,120 --> 00:09:01,980
Four days until Friday. And one thing

180
00:09:01,880 --> 00:09:04,140
in that box is still sealed.

181
00:09:04,852 --> 00:09:05,672
The card.

182
00:09:07,363 --> 00:09:10,603
That night, on Walter's front step, Lily

183
00:09:10,503 --> 00:09:16,143
opens the card. June 1st, 1998.

184
00:09:17,017 --> 00:09:19,837
Dear Lily. Today you were born, right

185
00:09:19,737 --> 00:09:21,797
next door. Your mom let me hold

186
00:09:21,697 --> 00:09:22,057
you.

187
00:09:22,782 --> 00:09:24,742
You grabbed one of my safety pins,

188
00:09:24,642 --> 00:09:26,682
and you would not let go.

189
00:09:27,335 --> 00:09:28,595
The safety pins...

190
00:09:29,417 --> 00:09:31,630
So today I bought you one thousand

191
00:09:31,530 --> 00:09:33,937
dollars of my fund. Don't touch it.

192
00:09:34,117 --> 00:09:35,237
Let it grow up with you.

193
00:09:35,621 --> 00:09:38,041
A receipt slides out. And this year's

194
00:09:37,941 --> 00:09:38,421
statement.

195
00:09:38,949 --> 00:09:41,829
Twenty-eight years later, that one thousand is

196
00:09:41,729 --> 00:09:43,689
worth $11,063.

197
00:09:44,894 --> 00:09:47,594
He planted this the day I was

198
00:09:47,494 --> 00:09:47,914
born.

199
00:09:48,555 --> 00:09:50,855
A sticky note, in newer ink:

200
00:09:51,607 --> 00:09:54,087
P.S. You brought me soup. I planted

201
00:09:53,987 --> 00:09:55,507
you a tree. Fair trade.

202
00:09:56,071 --> 00:09:57,851
I thought I was the one helping

203
00:09:57,751 --> 00:10:00,911
him. Sunday soup. Out of pity.

204
00:10:01,776 --> 00:10:05,076
He was helping me the whole time.

205
00:10:05,777 --> 00:10:06,617
And on the back:

206
00:10:07,487 --> 00:10:09,727
If Derek makes trouble, read him page

207
00:10:09,627 --> 00:10:10,027
one.

208
00:10:11,326 --> 00:10:15,686
Friday. Courtroom Two. Derek wears sunglasses indoors.

209
00:10:16,386 --> 00:10:17,346
And a captain's hat.

210
00:10:18,045 --> 00:10:21,445
I'm dressing for the life I'm about

211
00:10:21,345 --> 00:10:22,105
to have.

212
00:10:22,645 --> 00:10:25,105
Your Honor, here are Walter's own words.

213
00:10:25,465 --> 00:10:26,065
Page one.

214
00:10:26,605 --> 00:10:30,445
Walter's Rules. One: pay yourself first. Three

215
00:10:30,345 --> 00:10:32,485
hundred, every month, before anything else.

216
00:10:33,077 --> 00:10:35,957
Two: when the world panics, go shopping.

217
00:10:36,497 --> 00:10:37,317
Keep a storm jar.

218
00:10:37,956 --> 00:10:40,336
Three: don't touch it. Time does the

219
00:10:40,236 --> 00:10:40,956
heavy lifting.

220
00:10:41,637 --> 00:10:43,897
Four: a coat with safety pins is

221
00:10:43,797 --> 00:10:46,277
still a coat. Buy freedom, not stuff.

222
00:10:46,926 --> 00:10:49,366
Five: when I'm gone, it goes to

223
00:10:49,266 --> 00:10:51,666
the library and the hospital. They were

224
00:10:51,566 --> 00:10:52,266
good to me.

225
00:10:52,783 --> 00:10:55,323
The judge flips through the envelopes. Then

226
00:10:55,223 --> 00:10:56,263
takes off her glasses.

227
00:10:56,995 --> 00:10:58,715
A man who never missed a month

228
00:10:58,615 --> 00:11:01,155
in thirty-nine years? Very sound mind.

229
00:11:01,855 --> 00:11:02,795
The will stands.

230
00:11:05,022 --> 00:11:06,522
So I get nothing?

231
00:11:07,266 --> 00:11:09,906
Not nothing. Walter left you one item.

232
00:11:10,708 --> 00:11:13,748
A garment bag. The coat. Eleven safety

233
00:11:13,648 --> 00:11:14,228
pins.

234
00:11:14,940 --> 00:11:17,580
For Derek. It's warm. It's paid for.

235
00:11:17,900 --> 00:11:19,740
And it'll teach you more than money

236
00:11:19,640 --> 00:11:20,080
would.

237
00:11:20,573 --> 00:11:22,673
Derek puts it on. One safety pin

238
00:11:22,573 --> 00:11:24,073
pops. He catches it.

239
00:11:24,695 --> 00:11:26,415
...It does have nice pockets.

240
00:11:27,926 --> 00:11:31,466
Outside, kids run up the library steps.

241
00:11:32,177 --> 00:11:35,577
Walter wasn't poor. He wasn't saving to

242
00:11:35,477 --> 00:11:38,897
look rich. He was saving to be

243
00:11:38,797 --> 00:11:39,437
free.

244
00:11:40,316 --> 00:11:45,296
That night: dragon number three. And the

245
00:11:45,196 --> 00:11:45,916
kitchen scissors.

246
00:11:46,980 --> 00:11:48,280
Snip.

247
00:11:48,950 --> 00:11:52,270
One dragon down. Two to go. And

248
00:11:52,170 --> 00:11:57,070
next month, three hundred dollars. In an

249
00:11:56,970 --> 00:11:57,450
envelope.

250
00:11:58,651 --> 00:12:01,871
And a new jar on her shelf:

251
00:12:02,471 --> 00:12:03,091
Storm Jar.

252
00:12:04,013 --> 00:12:07,653
Sounds like a movie? Real people have

253
00:12:07,553 --> 00:12:08,353
done this.

254
00:12:09,071 --> 00:12:12,911
In 2014, a Vermont janitor left about

255
00:12:12,811 --> 00:12:15,751
eight million dollars, much of it to

256
00:12:15,651 --> 00:12:17,571
his town's library and hospital.

257
00:12:18,349 --> 00:12:21,629
Walter, Lily and Derek are made up,

258
00:12:21,989 --> 00:12:25,849
and so are their choices. The market

259
00:12:25,749 --> 00:12:29,309
returns are real, before fees and taxes.

260
00:12:30,413 --> 00:12:34,133
Past results don't promise the future. This

261
00:12:34,033 --> 00:12:37,493
is education, not financial advice.

262
00:12:38,338 --> 00:12:41,498
Now tell me in one word: could

263
00:12:41,398 --> 00:12:43,658
you live like Walter for ten years?

264
00:12:44,558 --> 00:12:46,578
Yes or no?

265
00:12:47,602 --> 00:12:52,602
I'd say yes. But I'm keeping the

266
00:12:52,502 --> 00:12:53,642
chicken in my soup.

267
00:12:54,214 --> 00:12:56,754
And send this to a friend who

268
00:12:56,654 --> 00:12:58,894
thinks small money doesn't matter.

269
00:12:59,700 --> 00:13:02,580
I'm Penny. See you next time.
